</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Causality of Intermediate Variables</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to determine whether the variables, <math id="A7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, we identify in models’ intermediate representations have a causal effect on the outcome. We describe a preliminary investigation below.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use activation patching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Vig et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, a common method for conducting causal interventions in interpretability, and patch representations across tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We first identify tasks with the same <math id="A7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> but different <math id="A7.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>, such as <span id="A7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">antonym-spanish</span> (<math id="A7.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\circ f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\circ f</annotation></semantics></math>) and <span id="A7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">antonym-german</span> (<math id="A7.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}\circ f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}\circ f</annotation></semantics></math>). For some <math id="A7.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract a single intermediate representation from the forward pass of <math id="A7.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> and patch it into the forward pass of <math id="A7.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math>. On average (over many <math id="A7.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>), we measure the causal effects on the predictions <math id="A7.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A7.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A7.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A7.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We extract the representation from <math id="A7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> at the position and layer where <math id="A7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A7.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(x)</annotation></semantics></math> have the highest reciprocal rank (and only use instances where this value is at least <math id="A7.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>). We patch this representation into the forward pass for <math id="A7.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> at the median location where intermediate values are highest (layer 18 and 71st percentile query token position; identified among variables that reach RR <math id="A7.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>). We apply this intervention to two groups: instances with intermediate values that reach a peak RR <math id="A7.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, instances with direct or compositional processing signatures.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A7.F13" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01685v1/patching_across_tasks_comp.svg" id="A7.F13.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:505/359;" width="505" height="359"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 13: </span>Causal effects on predicted values after patching from <math id="A7.F13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A7.F13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for instances with compositional processing signatures.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A7.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01685v1/patching_across_tasks_direct.svg" id="A7.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/114;" width="439" height="114"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 14: </span>Causal effects on predicted values after patching from <math id="A7.F14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A7.F14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> for instances with direct processing signatures.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Antonym–Translation tasks (which tend to have compositional signatures) show the most significant causal effect: on average, <math id="A7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> decrease by -0.95 and -0.4, and <math id="A7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> increase by 0.20 and 0.24. The effect on <math id="A7.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> clearly implicates the existence and causality of <math id="A7.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> in the patched activation; that on <math id="A7.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the additional existence of either itself or the function vector <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Todd et al., 2024</a>)</cite> for <math id="A7.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in that representation. The causal effects on compositional instances of <span id="A7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">product-company-hq</span> and <span id="A7.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">product-company-ceo</span> are smaller.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">But we can also see a clear difference between the causal effects on the compositional and direct instances. Indeed, the effects on <span id="A7.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">product-company-hq</span> and <span id="A7.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">product-company-ceo</span> are larger in their compositional instances. Patching activations from <span id="A7.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">plus-10-times-2</span> into <span id="A7.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">plus-100-times-2</span> primarily decreases <math id="A7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(f(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(f(x))</annotation></semantics></math> and increases <math id="A7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math>, perhaps only implying the existence of the representation for <math id="A7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>g</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\prime}(f(x^{\prime}))</annotation></semantics></math> in the patched activation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Linearity Correlations</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similarly to the experiment in <a href="#S5" title="5 Compositional Processing and Embedding Space Linearity ‣ How Do Language Models Compose Functions?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Secs.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.1 Experimental Design ‣ 5 Compositional Processing and Embedding Space Linearity ‣ How Do Language Models Compose Functions?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we investigate the relationship between our compositionality heuristic and embedding space linearity for variations of the hops (rather than of the compositional task).</p>
</div>
<figure id="A8.F15" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.01685v1/lens_hop_correlation.svg" id="A8.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:439/311;" width="439" height="311"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 15: </span>Relationships between presence of intermediate variables and embedding space linearity for the hops. We find weaker correlations in all cases. <math id="A8.F15.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2}=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2}=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> against the linearity of the first hop; <math id="A8.F15.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2}=0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2}=0.28</annotation></semantics></math> against the second hop; <math id="A8.F15.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2}=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2}=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> using the minimum linearity between the hops; and <math id="A8.F15.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{2}=0.20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0.20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{2}=0.20</annotation></semantics></math> using the maximum.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
